WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.180
The critics have seen the&nbsp;
fourth "John Wick" flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.960
and yeah, they're thinking he's back.&nbsp;
Here's what early reviews have to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.274
about the latest installment in&nbsp;
the unstoppable hitman franchise.

00:00:10.274 --> 00:00:13.006
“People keep asking if I’m back

00:00:13.006 --> 00:00:15.199
and I haven’t really had an answer.

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:19.038
But now, yeah. I’M THINKING I’M BACK.” 

00:00:19.038 --> 00:00:23.400
The much-anticipated "John Wick: Chapter&nbsp;
4" will hit movie theaters worldwide on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.920
March 24th, but the review embargo for the Keanu&nbsp;
Reeves-led blockbuster has already been lifted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.620
and the vast majority of critics are heaping&nbsp;
praise on it. The fourth iteration of the saga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.860
holds an 88% critic score on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;
with 26 reviews, as of the making of this video.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.280
"Chapter 4" is filled with things that&nbsp;
happen in every "John Wick" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.480
with the most important of those being&nbsp;
over-the-top combat sequences happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.980
at a breakneck pace. This time around,&nbsp;
director Chad Stahelski has taken viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.240
on a journey across a number of different&nbsp;
locations spread out across three continents.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.360
Clocking in at a whopping 162 minutes of runtime,&nbsp;
"Chapter 4" follows the titular character as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.280
strives to break free from The High Table once&nbsp;
and for all. Nearly three hours might seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.620
overkill for an action-focused film where the&nbsp;
plot plays second fiddle, but as most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:19.320
reviews suggest, it was a conscious decision on&nbsp;
Stahelski's part. Variety's Owen Gleiberman wrote,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.100
"Is 'Chapter 4' too long?&nbsp;
You bet it is. At moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.420
it's like an action film as liturgical&nbsp;
church service. Yet the movie is conceived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.740
as a knowingly overstuffed gift to 'John&nbsp;
Wick' fans, and on that level it succeeds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.980
It's like Sergio Leone crossed with&nbsp;
John Woo as seen in Times Square."

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.300
One thing that all of the "Chapter&nbsp;
4" reviewers highlighted, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.040
was the fact that the movie is by far the longest&nbsp;
in the franchise. They were full of praise for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.200
Stahelski's ability to keep viewers engaged for so&nbsp;
long while maintaining consistently high quality.

00:01:49.358 --> 00:01:53.558
IGN's Tom Jorgensen even called&nbsp;
it "a modern action masterclass,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.558 --> 00:01:57.180
and gave the movie a rare score of 10/10, writing,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:03.000
"In an age of increased grumbling about films with&nbsp;
two-plus-hour runtimes, Chapter 4's roaring pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.900
serves as a counter argument that proclaims&nbsp;
movies should be as long as they need to be."

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.820
Writing for BBC Culture, Caryn James praised&nbsp;
the visuals and choreography, writing,

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:17.160
"Knives, guns, and martial arts come into play,&nbsp;
often in lovely, red-tinged light to the sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.780
of glass shattering all around [...] John&nbsp;
Wick goes full James Bond, globe-trotting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.280
shooting his way through glamorous cities, with&nbsp;
action that is even more spectacularly staged."

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.738
Citing the gripping combat scenes and&nbsp;
praising the work of the stunt team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.738 --> 00:02:33.360
The Hollywood Reporter's Frank Sheck&nbsp;
wrote that "Chapter 4" "outdoes its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.860
predecessors in nearly every respect."&nbsp;
This sentiment prevails in other reviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.300
with Eric Eisenberg writing for CinemaBlend&nbsp;
that "there's a strong argument to be made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.620
that it's the best of the sequels," and&nbsp;
Digital Spy's Ian Sandwell summing it up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.940
as "not bad for a series that started out&nbsp;
with a hitman avenging his dead puppy."

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.440
According to most critics, the action in&nbsp;
"Chapter 4" is so good that it excuses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.460
shortcomings of its plot. At one point&nbsp;
in her review for Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.444
Leah Greenblatt posed a question:

00:03:01.444 --> 00:03:05.340
"How does nearly three hours&nbsp;
of wham-bam noise and nonsense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.896
unmitigated by any meaningful&nbsp;
plot, work as well as it does?"

00:03:08.896 --> 00:03:12.720
She went on to answer it by pointing&nbsp;
toward Keanu Reeves' ability to remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.494
likable despite playing a mass-murdering hitman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.494 --> 00:03:19.754
writing that there's, quote, "something ineffable&nbsp;
in Reeves that you can't help but root for."

00:03:19.754 --> 00:03:22.500
Nick Schager of the Daily Beast emphasized how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.380
actor expresses his character&nbsp;
without much dialogue, writing,

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.790
"Letting his stares and homicidal&nbsp;
skills do the talking for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.948 --> 00:03:33.278
Reeves exudes typically fearsome&nbsp;
gravity, purpose, and weariness."

00:03:33.278 --> 00:03:37.500
In a Sunshine State Cineplex review,&nbsp;
Alan French went further, writing,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.560
"Reeves shows why he's one of&nbsp;
the best genre actors in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.189
world and pours his heart and soul&nbsp;
into a soft-spoken performance."

00:03:44.274 --> 00:03:45.640
“Good luck, Mr. Wick.”

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:49.852
“Thanks, Doc.” 

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.100
Although he makes all the stunts&nbsp;
and fast-paced combat seem easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.140
Reeves admitted in a recent Fandango interview&nbsp;
that "Chapter 4" was the "hardest film he's ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.426
done," but based on early reviews, it&nbsp;
looks like it was worth all the effort.

